## Fix notes

The failure came from relying on `Limits.getLimitCallouts()`: **in test context this returns 0**, so `batchSize` became 0, the remainder never shrank, and the job chained itself infinitely → `System.AsyncException: Maximum stack depth has been reached` (this also explains why even `singleAccount` failed).

Fix: no more limit-dependent chaining. `run()` now deterministically splits the input into fixed chunks of **100 accounts** (the platform's callouts-per-transaction cap) and enqueues **one Queueable per chunk up front** — no self-chaining at all. A 200-account marketplace batch becomes exactly 2 jobs (well under the 50-enqueues-per-transaction limit), and each job makes at most 100 callouts, one HTTP request per account, exactly as before.

